== Directive Principles of State Policy & Fundamental Duties
- *DPSP (Part IV, Articles 36–51):* Borrowed from the Irish Constitution. Non-justiciable moral guidelines commanding the state to establish socio-economic democracy and a welfare state (e.g., Uniform Civil Code — Article 44; Organization of village panchayats — Article 40; Protection of environment and forests — Article 48A).
- *Fundamental Duties (Part IVA, Article 51A):* Incorporated via the 42nd Amendment Act (1976) based on the recommendations of the *Swaran Singh Committee*, inspired by the USSR Constitution. Originally 10 duties; the 11th duty (obligation of parents to provide education to children aged 6–14) was added by the 86th Amendment Act (2002).

== High-Frequency Constitutional and Statutory Bodies
- *Election Commission of India (Article 324):* Independent constitutional authority administering elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice President.
- *Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG, Article 148):* Guardian of the public purse; audits all receipts and expenditure of the Government of India and the states.
- *Union Public Service Commission (UPSC, Article 315):* Central recruitment authority.
- *Finance Commission (Article 280):* Constituted every five years by the President to recommend tax distribution between the Union and the States.
- *Statutory Bodies (Created by Act of Parliament):* National Human Rights Commission (NHRC, 1993), National Commission for Women (NCW, 1992).
- *Executive Body (Non-Constitutional, Non-Statutory):* *NITI Aayog* (National Institution for Transforming India), established via cabinet resolution on *1 January 2015*, replacing the erstwhile Planning Commission.

== Emergency Provisions (Part XVIII)
- *National Emergency (Article 352):* Proclaimed on grounds of war, external aggression, or armed rebellion. Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended during a National Emergency.
- *President's Rule / State Emergency (Article 356):* Imposed when governance in a state cannot be carried on in accordance with constitutional provisions.
- *Financial Emergency (Article 360):* Proclaimed if financial stability or credit of India is threatened. Notably, *Article 360 has never been invoked in India's history*.

